# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TekDoctor — cPanel Node.js App (Passenger) root .htaccess
#
# PLACE THIS FILE at the ROOT of your cPanel Node.js application folder,
# i.e. the same folder that contains server/server.js (NOT inside the folder
# that contains the built frontend assets).
#
# cPanel's "Setup Node.js App" normally GENERATES this file automatically.
# Use this only as a reference to confirm your cPanel app is configured
# correctly, or as a starting point if your host requires a manual file.
#
# A full cPanel/CloudLinux Passenger config looks like this (adjust paths):
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DO NOT REMOVE. CLOUDLINUX PASSENGER CONFIGURATION
# PassengerAppRoot "/home/thetekdoctor/backend"
# PassengerBaseURI "/"
# PassengerNodejs "/home/thetekdoctor/nodevenv/backend/24/bin/node"
# PassengerAppType node
# PassengerStartupFile server.js
# Require all granted
# Options -MultiViews
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# NOTE: Do NOT place the SPA rewrite/fallback rules (the ones that map every
# route to index.html) in this folder. In the Passenger model, Express
# (server.js) performs the SPA fallback itself. Adding Apache rewrite rules
# here can conflict with Passenger routing.
#
# Also: the built frontend assets (client/dist) should NOT carry an Apache
# SPA-routing .htaccess when served via Passenger — Express already handles
# client-side routing.
